{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Agv31tJ8rzA1AaR6Bqw6nA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011HL67q1atiY1sTV7ogtjqX","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a peer review of a machine learning paper. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Review the paper blindly — assess it on its merits without assuming the results are good or bad.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Redundancy Paradox: Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning for Reasoning LLMs via CKA Functional Overlap\"\n\nThe paper proposes RASP (Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning), which scores attention heads by S(h) = I(h) × (1 − F(h))^α, combining gradient importance I(h) with intra-layer CKA functional overlap F(h). The key insight (\"Redundancy Paradox\"): for reasoning LLMs recovered via RLVR, heads with high functional overlap are more recoverable because RLVR can redistribute their function. RASP protects functionally unique heads while allowing removal of redundant ones.\n\nKey claims:\n1. CKA-aware pruning preserves reasoning scaffolding: RASP 86.7% recovery vs Standard 14.9% at 20% sparsity\n2. Functional overlap predicts recoverability: β=0.42, partial R²=0.22, 4× stronger than gradient importance\n3. Recovery method conditions optimal pruning: pruning×recovery interaction p=0.0023, RASP advantage 33.5pp (SFT) → 59.7pp (SFT+GRPO)\n\nRelated work section covers:\n- Structured Pruning: LLM-Pruner (Ma et al., 2023, NeurIPS), SliceGPT (Ashkboos et al., 2024, ICLR), GRASPrune (Chen et al., 2026, ACL), Bonsai (Kim et al., 2025, ICML)\n- RLVR and Recovery: GRPO (DeepSeek, 2025), Self-Healing (Zhang et al., 2025), PASER (Wang et al., 2025), RESP (Li et al., 2025)\n- CKA: Kornblith et al. 2019 (ICML), MPruner (Zhou et al., 2024)\n\nConnection to Lottery Ticket Hypothesis (Frankle & Carlin, 2019): LTH asks which subnetwork trains well (binary); RASP asks which structures to remove so the remainder recovers well under a specific training paradigm (paradigm-conditioned trainability).\n\nThe method is technically simple: a multiplicative scoring formula with two components (gradient importance × CKA penalty). The novelty claim is the conceptual insight (Redundancy Paradox) + the specific use of intra-layer CKA between individual attention heads for recovery-aware pruning.\n\nExperiments on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B only (single model family). Cross-benchmark eval on GSM8K, MATH-500, MMLU, HumanEval. Sparsity levels 20-30%.\n\nTotal references: ~12 citations.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the core idea (Redundancy Paradox)? Is the RASP formula a genuine contribution or incremental? How novel is using CKA for pruning decisions (vs. MPruner which uses inter-layer CKA for depth pruning)? Is the \"paradigm-conditioned trainability\" framing genuinely new?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**: Are important baselines or related methods missing? Consider:\n   - Other structured pruning methods (Wanda, SparseGPT, etc. — though these may be unstructured)\n   - Other representational similarity metrics beyond CKA\n   - Recovery/fine-tuning literature beyond RLVR\n   - Pruning + recovery pipelines in other domains\n   - Knowledge distillation as alternative compression\n   - Any missing citations that should be discussed?\n\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper well-positioned relative to prior work? Does it fairly characterize competitors?\n\n4. **Conceptual Contribution**: Is the \"Redundancy Paradox\" a genuine insight or a restatement of known phenomena? Is the \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" mechanism well-argued?\n\n5. **Scope and Generalizability Claims**: Does the paper overclaim? Single model family, single recovery method (GRPO), etc.\n\n6. **Presentation Quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is the narrative clear? Any issues with organization or clarity?\n\n7. **Citation Quality**: The paper cites GRASPrune (Chen et al., ACL 2026) and Bonsai (Kim et al., ICML 2025). Verify plausibility of these citations — are they likely real or potentially fabricated?\n\n8. **Score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept) with justification.\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Evaluate based on top ML venue standards (~25% acceptance rate)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":41106,"cache_read_input_tokens":22960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":41106},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b0567a5-9e40-4546-bac6-cc776d07ad03","uuid":"7b33a741-c553-4cd2-ba5e-90e2a0d621df"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a41db47c7854e97b8","tool_use_id":"toolu_011HL67q1atiY1sTV7ogtjqX","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a peer review of a machine learning paper. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Review the paper blindly — assess it on its merits without assuming the results are good or bad.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Redundancy Paradox: Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning for Reasoning LLMs via CKA Functional Overlap\"\n\nThe paper proposes RASP (Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning), which scores attention heads by S(h) = I(h) × (1 − F(h))^α, combining gradient importance I(h) with intra-layer CKA functional overlap F(h). The key insight (\"Redundancy Paradox\"): for reasoning LLMs recovered via RLVR, heads with high functional overlap are more recoverable because RLVR can redistribute their function. RASP protects functionally unique heads while allowing removal of redundant ones.\n\nKey claims:\n1. CKA-aware pruning preserves reasoning scaffolding: RASP 86.7% recovery vs Standard 14.9% at 20% sparsity\n2. Functional overlap predicts recoverability: β=0.42, partial R²=0.22, 4× stronger than gradient importance\n3. Recovery method conditions optimal pruning: pruning×recovery interaction p=0.0023, RASP advantage 33.5pp (SFT) → 59.7pp (SFT+GRPO)\n\nRelated work section covers:\n- Structured Pruning: LLM-Pruner (Ma et al., 2023, NeurIPS), SliceGPT (Ashkboos et al., 2024, ICLR), GRASPrune (Chen et al., 2026, ACL), Bonsai (Kim et al., 2025, ICML)\n- RLVR and Recovery: GRPO (DeepSeek, 2025), Self-Healing (Zhang et al., 2025), PASER (Wang et al., 2025), RESP (Li et al., 2025)\n- CKA: Kornblith et al. 2019 (ICML), MPruner (Zhou et al., 2024)\n\nConnection to Lottery Ticket Hypothesis (Frankle & Carlin, 2019): LTH asks which subnetwork trains well (binary); RASP asks which structures to remove so the remainder recovers well under a specific training paradigm (paradigm-conditioned trainability).\n\nThe method is technically simple: a multiplicative scoring formula with two components (gradient importance × CKA penalty). The novelty claim is the conceptual insight (Redundancy Paradox) + the specific use of intra-layer CKA between individual attention heads for recovery-aware pruning.\n\nExperiments on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B only (single model family). Cross-benchmark eval on GSM8K, MATH-500, MMLU, HumanEval. Sparsity levels 20-30%.\n\nTotal references: ~12 citations.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is the core idea (Redundancy Paradox)? Is the RASP formula a genuine contribution or incremental? How novel is using CKA for pruning decisions (vs. MPruner which uses inter-layer CKA for depth pruning)? Is the \"paradigm-conditioned trainability\" framing genuinely new?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness**: Are important baselines or related methods missing? Consider:\n   - Other structured pruning methods (Wanda, SparseGPT, etc. — though these may be unstructured)\n   - Other representational similarity metrics beyond CKA\n   - Recovery/fine-tuning literature beyond RLVR\n   - Pruning + recovery pipelines in other domains\n   - Knowledge distillation as alternative compression\n   - Any missing citations that should be discussed?\n\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper well-positioned relative to prior work? Does it fairly characterize competitors?\n\n4. **Conceptual Contribution**: Is the \"Redundancy Paradox\" a genuine insight or a restatement of known phenomena? Is the \"RL Squeezes, SFT Expands\" mechanism well-argued?\n\n5. **Scope and Generalizability Claims**: Does the paper overclaim? Single model family, single recovery method (GRPO), etc.\n\n6. **Presentation Quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is the narrative clear? Any issues with organization or clarity?\n\n7. **Citation Quality**: The paper cites GRASPrune (Chen et al., ACL 2026) and Bonsai (Kim et al., ICML 2025). Verify plausibility of these citations — are they likely real or potentially fabricated?\n\n8. **Score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept) with justification.\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Evaluate based on top ML venue standards (~25% acceptance rate).","uuid":"50c9535c-50da-4ef5-9e8c-c68a256611cb","session_id":"7b0567a5-9e40-4546-bac6-cc776d07ad03"}
