{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Axg5XoJtL7VBvTp4zwzVYu","type":"tool_result","content":"200\t\n201\tRASP recovers to 72.1\\% (86.7\\% of baseline), while Standard recovers only 12.4\\% (14.9\\%).\n202\tThe 59.7pp gap between recovered models (Cohen's $h = 1.28$) represents a qualitative difference between recoverable and unrecoverable compression.\n203\tCritically, the recovery gap (59.7pp) is more than double the post-prune gap (27.5pp), demonstrating that the recovery pipeline \\emph{amplifies} structural choices.\n204\t\n205\tGRASPrune provides the key intermediate baseline.\n206\tDespite having arguably better importance estimation (learned gates vs.\\ static Taylor), GRASPrune recovers to only 41.3\\% (49.6\\% of baseline)---30.8pp below RASP.\n207\tThe post-prune gap is 11.1pp, but the recovery gap is 30.8pp, quantifying the ``CKA premium'': what CKA-awareness adds beyond good importance estimation.\n208\tThis rules out the confound that RASP's advantage is simply better importance scoring.\n209\t\n210\tBonsai (20.1\\% recovery) and Standard (14.9\\%) confirm that gradient-free vs.\\ gradient-based importance is a minor distinction compared to CKA-aware vs.\\ CKA-unaware pruning.\n211\t\n212\t\n213\t\\subsection{Recovery Pipeline Analysis}\n214\t\\label{sec:pipeline}\n215\t\n216\tTable~\\ref{tab:pipeline} decomposes the recovery pipeline into its two stages.\n217\t\n218\t\\begin{table}[t]\n219\t\\centering\n220\t\\caption{Recovery pipeline stages for RASP and Standard at 20\\% sparsity.}\n221\t\\label{tab:pipeline}\n222\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n223\t\\toprule\n224\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Post-Prune (\\%)} & \\textbf{Post-SFT (\\%)} & \\textbf{SFT Gain (pp)} & \\textbf{EOS Rate (\\%)} \\\\\n225\t\\midrule\n226\tRASP     & 28.9 & 48.2 & +19.3 & 97 \\\\\n227\tStandard & 1.4  & 14.7 & +13.3 & 93 \\\\\n228\t\\bottomrule\n229\t\\end{tabular}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\tSFT warmup serves two functions: (1) fixing generation pathology (no-EOS infinite loops), achieving $>93\\%$ EOS rate for both methods, and (2) providing a reasoning template for subsequent GRPO.\n233\tRASP's training loss converges to 0.82 vs.\\ 1.41 for Standard, reflecting the structural capacity difference.\n234\t\n235\tThe critical stage is GRPO: it contributes +23.9pp for RASP (48.2$\\to$72.1\\%) but $-$2.3pp for Standard (14.7$\\to$12.4\\%).\n236\tGRPO's mean reward reaches 0.68 for RASP vs.\\ 0.11 for Standard.\n237\tRASP's higher KL divergence (3.2 vs.\\ 0.9) indicates genuine policy movement---the model explores and finds better reasoning paths.\n238\tStandard's low KL means it barely moves from SFT initialization because exploration is unrewarding: the destroyed reasoning scaffold provides no substrate for GRPO to optimize.\n239\t\n240\t\n241\t\\subsection{Scoring Ablation}\n242\t\\label{sec:ablation}\n243\t\n244\tTo isolate the contribution of each scoring component, we evaluate five ablated variants at 20\\% sparsity (Table~\\ref{tab:ablation}).\n245\t\n246\t\\begin{table}[t]\n247\t\\centering\n248\t\\caption{Scoring ablation at 20\\% sparsity. $I(h)$: gradient importance. $F(h)$: CKA overlap.}\n249\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n250\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n251\t\\toprule\n252\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Formula} & \\textbf{GSM8K (\\%)} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} \\\\\n253\t\\midrule\n254\tRASP (full)      & $I(h) \\times (1-F(h))^\\alpha$ & 28.9 & {[26.5, 31.4]} \\\\\n255\tCKA-only         & $(1-F(h))^\\alpha$             & 21.3 & {[19.1, 23.7]} \\\\\n256\tRandom           & Uniform random                & 6.7  & {[4.9, 8.9]} \\\\\n257\tMagnitude-only   & $\\|w_h\\|_2$                   & 3.8  & {[2.3, 5.8]} \\\\\n258\tGradient-only    & $\\|w_h \\odot \\nabla w_h\\|_2$  & 1.4  & {[0.7, 2.4]} \\\\\n259\t\\bottomrule\n260\t\\end{tabular}\n261\t\\end{table}\n262\t\n263\tThe ablation provides direct evidence for the Redundancy Paradox: importance-based pruning (gradient: 1.4\\%, magnitude: 3.8\\%) is \\emph{worse than random} (6.7\\%) for reasoning model compression.\n264\tThis occurs because importance metrics are anti-correlated with functional uniqueness in reasoning LLMs---``important'' heads tend to be high-overlap heads that contribute broadly, while ``unimportant'' heads may encode specialized reasoning operations.\n265\t\n266\tCKA-only scoring (21.3\\%) vastly outperforms all importance variants, confirming that functional overlap is the primary signal for reasoning preservation.\n267\tThe full RASP score (28.9\\%) outperforms CKA-only by 7.6pp, showing that importance weighting correctly identifies which unique structures are also functionally important---pure CKA protection without importance wastes budget retaining unique-but-useless structures.\n268\t\n269\t\n270\t\\subsection{Per-Structure Recoverability Analysis}\n271\t\\label{sec:recoverability}\n272\t\n273\tWe conduct a mixed-effects regression on all 896 attention heads, predicting post-recovery activation change (recoverability proxy) from CKA overlap $F(h)$, gradient importance $I(h)$, weight norm $\\|w_h\\|_2$, and activation magnitude, with layer as random effect (Table~\\ref{tab:regression}).\n274\t\n275\t\\begin{table}[t]\n276\t\\centering\n277\t\\caption{Mixed-effects regression on per-head recoverability ($N = 896$ heads, 32 layers). $\\beta$: standardized coefficient. Partial $R^2$: unique variance explained.}\n278\t\\label{tab:regression}\n279\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n280\t\\toprule\n281\t\\textbf{Predictor} & $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ & \\textbf{SE} & \\textbf{$t$} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Partial $R^2$} \\\\\n282\t\\midrule\n283\t$F(h)$ (CKA overlap) & 0.42 & 0.06 & 7.00 & $<$0.0001 & 0.22 \\\\\n284\t$I(h)$ (gradient imp.) & 0.11 & 0.05 & 2.13 & 0.034 & 0.04 \\\\\n285\t$\\|w_h\\|_2$ (weight norm) & 0.08 & 0.05 & 1.55 & 0.12 & 0.01 \\\\\n286\tAct.\\ magnitude & 0.05 & 0.05 & 1.02 & 0.31 & $<$0.01 \\\\\n287\t\\midrule\n288\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{Marginal $R^2$} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{0.37} \\\\\n289\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{Conditional $R^2$} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{0.58} \\\\\n290\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{ICC (layer)} & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{0.21} \\\\\n291\t\\bottomrule\n292\t\\end{tabular}\n293\t\\end{table}\n294\t\n295\tCKA overlap ($\\beta = 0.42$, $p < 0.0001$) is the dominant predictor, 4$\\times$ stronger than gradient importance ($\\beta = 0.11$) and uniquely explaining 22\\% of recoverability variance.\n296\tThe marginal $R^2 = 0.37$ indicates that CKA plus standard metrics explain 37\\% of recovery outcomes---substantial for a per-head analysis.\n297\tThe conditional $R^2 = 0.58$ reveals additional layer-level structure: the ICC of 0.21 indicates non-trivial clustering, with middle layers (10--20) showing the strongest CKA-recoverability coupling, consistent with RLVR redistribution concentrating in the reasoning computation core.\n298\t\n299\tDiagnostic tests confirm model validity: VIF $< 3$ (no multicollinearity), Durbin-Watson $= 1.89$ (no autocorrelation), Breusch-Pagan $p = 0.18$ (homoscedastic residuals).\n300\t\n301\t\n302\t\\subsection{Pruning $\\times$ Recovery Method Interaction}\n303\t\\label{sec:interaction}\n304\t\n305\tTo test whether recovery method conditions optimal pruning, we compare the same pruned models under SFT-only vs.\\ SFT+GRPO recovery with identical data budgets (Table~\\ref{tab:interaction}).\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[t]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\caption{Pruning $\\times$ recovery method interaction (2$\\times$2 design) at 20\\% sparsity.}\n310\t\\label{tab:interaction}\n311\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n312\t\\toprule\n313\t\\textbf{Pruning} & \\textbf{SFT-Only (\\%)} & \\textbf{SFT+GRPO (\\%)} & \\textbf{GRPO Marginal (pp)} \\\\\n314\t\\midrule\n315\tRASP     & 48.2 & 72.1 & +23.9 \\\\\n316\tStandard & 14.7 & 12.4 & $-$2.3 \\\\\n317\t\\midrule\n318\tRASP Advantage (pp) & 33.5 & 59.7 & --- \\\\\n319\t\\bottomrule\n320\t\\end{tabular}\n321\t\\end{table}\n322\t\n323\t\\begin{table}[t]\n324\t\\centering\n325\t\\caption{Interaction test statistics.}\n326\t\\label{tab:interaction_stats}\n327\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n328\t\\toprule\n329\t\\textbf{Effect} & \\textbf{Estimate (pp)} & \\textbf{SE} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n330\t\\midrule\n331\tPruning (RASP vs Standard) & 46.6 & 3.2 & $<$0.0001 \\\\\n332\tMethod (GRPO vs SFT) & 10.8 & 3.1 & 0.0006 \\\\\n333\tPruning $\\times$ Method & 26.2 & 4.5 & 0.0023 \\\\\n334\t\\bottomrule\n335\t\\end{tabular}\n336\t\\end{table}\n337\t\n338\tThe interaction effect is significant ($p = 0.0023$, 26.2pp).\n339\tUnder SFT-only recovery, RASP's advantage is 33.5pp---substantial but reflecting a quantitative difference.\n340\tUnder SFT+GRPO, it explodes to 59.7pp---a qualitative difference.\n341\tThe mechanism is clear: SFT recovery works by expanding correct paths through imitation and benefits from any preserved structure without strong dependence on functional redundancy patterns.\n342\tGRPO recovery works by compressing incorrect paths and reinforcing correct ones through exploration---it requires functional redundancy (overlapping structures whose function can be redistributed) to find alternative reasoning routes.\n343\tRASP preserves this redundancy by design; Standard destroys it.\n344\t\n345\tThe negative GRPO marginal for Standard ($-$2.3pp) strengthens this interpretation: GRPO does not merely fail to help Standard-pruned models---it actively destabilizes them.\n346\tThe KL penalty erodes SFT-learned patterns without discovering better alternatives, because the structural prerequisites for RL-based exploration are absent.\n347\t\n348\t\n349\t\\subsection{Sparsity Scaling}\n350\t\\label{sec:sparsity}\n351\t\n352\tWe evaluate RASP and Standard across three sparsity levels (Table~\\ref{tab:sparsity}) to map the operating envelope.\n353\t\n354\t\\begin{table}[t]\n355\t\\centering\n356\t\\caption{Sparsity scaling for RASP and Standard. Recovery shown where available.}\n357\t\\label{tab:sparsity}\n358\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n359\t\\toprule\n360\t\\textbf{Sparsity} & \\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Post-Prune (\\%)} & \\textbf{Recovered (\\%)} & \\textbf{Recovery Rate (\\%)} & \\textbf{Status} \\\\\n361\t\\midrule\n362\t20\\% & RASP     & 28.9 & 72.1 & 86.7 & Recoverable \\\\\n363\t20\\% & Standard & 1.4  & 12.4 & 14.9 & Collapsed \\\\\n364\t\\midrule\n365\t25\\% & RASP     & 18.4 & 58.1 & 69.8 & Recoverable \\\\\n366\t25\\% & Standard & 0.3  & 5.4  & 6.5  & Collapsed \\\\\n367\t\\midrule\n368\t30\\% & RASP     & 6.2  & ---  & ---  & Near-collapse \\\\\n369\t30\\% & Standard & 0.1  & ---  & ---  & Collapsed \\\\\n370\t\\bottomrule\n371\t\\end{tabular}\n372\t\\end{table}\n373\t\n374\tRASP maintains meaningful recoverability at 25\\% sparsity (69.8\\% recovery rate, 58.1\\% accuracy), while Standard collapses at all tested sparsity levels.\n375\tAt 30\\%, RASP drops to 6.2\\% post-prune---approaching the collapse zone.\n376\tAt 35\\% (from a preliminary probe), both methods produce zero accuracy.\n377\t\n378\tThese results define RASP's practical operating range: 20\\% maximizes recovery quality (86.7\\%), 25\\% provides aggressive compression with substantial recovery (69.8\\%), and beyond 30\\% the model's inherent redundancy budget is exhausted.\n379\tThe DeepSeek-R1-Distill model, having been distilled from a larger model, has less structural redundancy than a natively-trained model, which bounds the compression ceiling.\n380\t\n381\t\n382\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Generalization}\n383\t\\label{sec:generalization}\n384\t\n385\tWe evaluate the best recovered model (RASP at 20\\%, SFT+GRPO) on three additional benchmarks to test whether recovery generalizes beyond GSM8K (Table~\\ref{tab:cross}).\n386\t\n387\t\\begin{table}[t]\n388\t\\centering\n389\t\\caption{Cross-benchmark evaluation of RASP-recovered model (20\\% sparsity, SFT+GRPO).}\n390\t\\label{tab:cross}\n391\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n392\t\\toprule\n393\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Baseline (\\%)} & \\textbf{RASP Recovered (\\%)} & \\textbf{Retention (\\%)} \\\\\n394\t\\midrule\n395\tGSM8K     & 83.2 & 72.1 & 86.7 \\\\\n396\tMATH-500  & 68.2 & 57.1 & 83.7 \\\\\n397\tMMLU      & 63.1 & 58.4 & 92.6 \\\\\n398\tHumanEval & 52.4 & 45.1 & 86.1 \\\\\n399\t\\midrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011HL67q1atiY1sTV7ogtjqX","session_id":"7b0567a5-9e40-4546-bac6-cc776d07ad03","uuid":"2605713c-3aa7-4caf-8330-10e31c9fc243","timestamp":"2026-05-15T00:15:04.743Z"}
