{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019jzj5GESdHU8sNAyXahtBd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AUh3EDAgdeJCFizS5mpXJP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_17/projects/recovery_aware_struct_prune/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T233950/best/paper.tex","offset":399,"limit":150},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4438,"cache_read_input_tokens":49751,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4438,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":35,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011HL67q1atiY1sTV7ogtjqX","session_id":"7b0567a5-9e40-4546-bac6-cc776d07ad03","uuid":"cb381d3f-94ff-4025-83b0-8a022b9ac360"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AUh3EDAgdeJCFizS5mpXJP","type":"tool_result","content":"399\t\\midrule\n400\t\\textbf{Mean} & --- & --- & \\textbf{87.3} \\\\\n401\t\\bottomrule\n402\t\\end{tabular}\n403\t\\end{table}\n404\t\n405\tRASP recovery generalizes consistently across diverse reasoning tasks with mean retention of 87.3\\%.\n406\tMMLU retention is highest (92.6\\%) because it primarily tests knowledge retrieval, which is less affected by structural pruning than multi-step reasoning.\n407\tMATH-500 retention is slightly lower (83.7\\%) because it requires deeper reasoning chains and more diverse mathematical operations.\n408\tHumanEval (86.1\\%) is comparable to GSM8K, as code generation requires similar multi-step reasoning.\n409\t\n410\tThe consistency across benchmarks confirms that RASP preserves the model's general reasoning scaffolding, not just arithmetic-specific patterns---the recovery is not overfitting to GSM8K training data.\n411\t\n412\t\n413\t\\subsection{Robustness Analysis}\n414\t\\label{sec:robustness}\n415\t\n416\t\\paragraph{Multi-Seed Stability.}\n417\tWe run RASP pruning with three calibration seeds (Table~\\ref{tab:seeds}).\n418\t\n419\t\\begin{table}[t]\n420\t\\centering\n421\t\\caption{Multi-seed robustness of RASP at 20\\% sparsity.}\n422\t\\label{tab:seeds}\n423\t\\begin{tabular}{ccc}\n424\t\\toprule\n425\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{GSM8K (\\%)} & \\textbf{Pruned-Set Overlap w/ Seed 42 (\\%)} \\\\\n426\t\\midrule\n427\t42  & 28.9 & --- \\\\\n428\t123 & 28.1 & 91 \\\\\n429\t7   & 30.2 & 89 \\\\\n430\t\\midrule\n431\t\\multicolumn{1}{l}{Mean $\\pm$ Std} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{29.1 $\\pm$ 1.1} \\\\\n432\t\\bottomrule\n433\t\\end{tabular}\n434\t\\end{table}\n435\t\n436\tThe standard deviation of 1.1pp across seeds confirms that RASP's structure selection is robust to calibration sample variation.\n437\tThe 89--91\\% overlap in pruned head sets shows that the same core heads are pruned regardless of seed, with only 16--20 borderline heads varying.\n438\t\n439\t\\paragraph{CKA Scoring Reliability.}\n440\tSplit-half consistency yields Spearman $\\rho = 0.94$ ($p < 0.0001$) across 896 heads.\n441\tCalibration size sensitivity is low: 256 samples yield 27.4\\% GSM8K (vs.\\ 28.9\\% at 512, 29.3\\% at 1024), with rank correlations of 0.91 (256 vs.\\ 512) and 0.97 (512 vs.\\ 1024).\n442\tThe 512-sample default is near-optimal, with diminishing returns beyond.\n443\t\n444\t\n445\t% ============================================================\n446\t% 6. DISCUSSION\n447\t% ============================================================\n448\t\\section{Discussion}\n449\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n450\t\n451\t\\paragraph{Why does importance-based pruning fail for reasoning models?}\n452\tThe scoring ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:ablation}) reveals that importance metrics (gradient, magnitude) produce \\emph{worse-than-random} pruning for reasoning preservation.\n453\tThe explanation is that importance scores in reasoning LLMs are anti-correlated with functional uniqueness: heads that contribute most to average-case loss tend to be high-overlap heads whose function is broadly distributed.\n454\tRemoving ``unimportant'' heads preferentially eliminates functionally unique structures that carry specialized reasoning operations.\n455\t\n456\t\\paragraph{The ``RL Squeezes, SFT Expands'' mechanism.}\n457\tThe pruning$\\times$recovery interaction (\\S\\ref{sec:interaction}) provides empirical support for this mechanism.\n458\tSFT expands correct paths through imitation---it benefits from any preserved structure proportionally to capacity.\n459\tGRPO squeezes by compressing incorrect paths and discovering alternatives through exploration---this requires functional redundancy (overlapping structures) as the substrate for redistribution.\n460\tStandard pruning, by removing high-overlap structures, eliminates precisely this substrate.\n461\t\n462\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n463\tOur experiments use a single model family (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B).\n464\tWhile cross-benchmark evaluation shows generalization across tasks, validation on other reasoning model families (e.g., QwQ, natively-trained reasoning models) remains future work.\n465\tThe recovery pipeline requires non-trivial compute (SFT + 2K GRPO steps); whether cheaper recovery methods preserve the structural advantage is an open question.\n466\tDistilled models may have less structural redundancy than natively-trained ones, potentially shifting RASP's operating envelope.\n467\t\n468\t\n469\t% ============================================================\n470\t% 7. CONCLUSION\n471\t% ============================================================\n472\t\\section{Conclusion}\n473\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n474\t\n475\tWe identified the Redundancy Paradox in structured pruning of reasoning LLMs: what is safe to remove for immediate accuracy is different from what is safe to remove for post-recovery accuracy.\n476\tRecovery-Aware Structured Pruning (RASP) resolves this by incorporating CKA functional overlap into the pruning score, protecting functionally unique reasoning scaffolding while permitting removal of structures whose function RLVR can redistribute.\n477\t\n478\tAt 20\\% sparsity, RASP enables 86.7\\% recovery of baseline reasoning accuracy, vs.\\ 14.9\\% for standard pruning and 49.6\\% for GRASPrune---establishing CKA-awareness as the critical ingredient for recovery-optimal compression.\n479\tPer-structure CKA overlap is the dominant predictor of recoverability ($\\beta = 0.42$, partial $R^2 = 0.22$), and the optimal pruning strategy depends on the recovery method (interaction $p = 0.0023$).\n480\tThese results generalize across sparsity levels and benchmarks (mean retention 87.3\\%).\n481\t\n482\tRASP reframes structural redundancy from a flaw to eliminate into a signal of recoverability, connecting structured pruning to the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis at a new level: paradigm-conditioned trainability.\n483\t\n484\t\n485\t% ============================================================\n486\t% REFERENCES\n487\t% ============================================================\n488\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n489\t\n490\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n491\t\n492\t\\bibitem[DeepSeek(2025)]{deepseek2025r1}\n493\tDeepSeek-AI.\n494\t\\newblock DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing reasoning capability in LLMs via reinforcement learning.\n495\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.12948}, 2025.\n496\t\n497\t\\bibitem[Qwen(2025)]{qwen2025qwq}\n498\tQwen Team.\n499\t\\newblock QwQ: Reflect deeply on the boundaries of the unknown.\n500\t\\newblock \\emph{Blog post}, 2025.\n501\t\n502\t\\bibitem[Ma et~al.(2023)]{llmpruner2023}\n503\tXinyin Ma, Gongfan Fang, and Xinchao Wang.\n504\t\\newblock LLM-Pruner: On the structural pruning of large language models.\n505\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n506\t\n507\t\\bibitem[Ashkboos et~al.(2024)]{slicegpt2024}\n508\tSaleh Ashkboos, Maximilian~L. Croci, Marcelo~Gennari do~Nascimento, Torsten Hoefler, and James Hensman.\n509\t\\newblock SliceGPT: Compress large language models by deleting rows and columns.\n510\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n511\t\n512\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2026)]{graspune2026}\n513\tWei Chen, Zhiyuan Liu, and Maosong Sun.\n514\t\\newblock GRASPrune: Gradient-aware structured pruning with differentiable gates.\n515\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2026.\n516\t\n517\t\\bibitem[Kim et~al.(2025)]{bonsai2025}\n518\tSehoon Kim, Sheng Shen, David Hooper, Amir Gholami, and Kurt Keutzer.\n519\t\\newblock Bonsai: Gradient-free structured pruning of large language models.\n520\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2025.\n521\t\n522\t\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2025)]{selfhealing2025}\n523\tYiming Zhang, Jianqiao Lu, and Zhiwei Jia.\n524\t\\newblock Self-healing in reasoning models: RLVR recovers pruned performance.\n525\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n526\t\n527\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2025)]{paser2025}\n528\tJiaxin Wang, Yuqing Yang, and Tao Lin.\n529\t\\newblock PASER: Pruning-aware sample selection for efficient recovery.\n530\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n531\t\n532\t\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2025)]{resp2025}\n533\tShiyang Li, Yao Fu, and Noah~A. Smith.\n534\t\\newblock RESP: Reasoning-calibrated structured pruning for LLMs.\n535\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n536\t\n537\t\\bibitem[Kornblith et~al.(2019)]{kornblith2019cka}\n538\tSimon Kornblith, Mohammad Norouzi, Honglak Lee, and Geoffrey Hinton.\n539\t\\newblock Similarity of neural network representations revisited.\n540\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2019.\n541\t\n542\t\\bibitem[Zhou et~al.(2024)]{mpruner2024}\n543\tYanqi Zhou, Nan Du, and Yanping Huang.\n544\t\\newblock MPruner: CKA-guided model pruning for efficient deployment.\n545\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n546\t\n547\t\\bibitem[Frankle and Carlin(2019)]{frankle2019lottery}\n548\tJonathan Frankle and Michael Carlin."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011HL67q1atiY1sTV7ogtjqX","session_id":"7b0567a5-9e40-4546-bac6-cc776d07ad03","uuid":"4a37b47a-97db-469b-a4c9-7e5b38250b1c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T00:15:14.721Z"}
