{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SyHMf3ZhzetfHL2vJp3dv7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DxE9NakQSDTxFnH2TCn5n4","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 retry: Methods + Experiments","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a peer review of a machine learning paper. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Review the paper blindly — assess it on its merits without assuming the results are good or bad.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Redundancy Paradox: Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning for Reasoning LLMs via CKA Functional Overlap\"\n\nThe paper proposes RASP (Recovery-Aware Structured Pruning), which scores attention heads by S(h) = I(h) × (1 − F(h))^α, combining gradient importance I(h) with intra-layer CKA functional overlap F(h). The key insight (\"Redundancy Paradox\"): for reasoning LLMs recovered via RLVR (reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards), heads with high functional overlap are more recoverable because RLVR can redistribute their function. RASP protects functionally unique heads while allowing removal of redundant ones.\n\nExperiments on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B at 20% structured sparsity:\n- Table 1 (Main results): RASP 72.1% post-GRPO (86.7% recovery), GRASPrune 41.3% (49.6%), Bonsai 16.7% (20.1%), Standard 12.4% (14.9%). Baseline 83.2%.\n- Table 2 (Pipeline stages): RASP post-SFT 48.2% (+19.3pp), Standard post-SFT 14.7% (+13.3pp). GRPO adds +23.9pp for RASP but -2.3pp for Standard.\n- Table 3 (Scoring ablation): RASP 28.9%, CKA-only 21.3%, Random 6.7%, Magnitude 3.8%, Gradient 1.4%.\n- Table 4 (Mixed-effects regression, N=896 heads): CKA overlap β=0.42 (p<0.0001, partial R²=0.22), gradient importance β=0.11 (p=0.034, partial R²=0.04). Marginal R²=0.37, Conditional R²=0.58, ICC=0.21.\n- Table 5 (Pruning×Recovery interaction): RASP advantage 33.5pp under SFT-only, 59.7pp under SFT+GRPO. Interaction p=0.0023, 26.2pp.\n- Table 6 (Interaction test): Pruning main effect 46.6pp (p<0.0001), Method main effect 10.8pp (p=0.0006), Interaction 26.2pp (p=0.0023).\n- Table 7 (Sparsity scaling): 20% RASP 72.1% recovered (86.7%), 25% RASP 58.1% recovered (69.8%), 30% RASP 6.2% post-prune (near-collapse). Standard collapsed at all levels.\n- Table 8 (Cross-benchmark): GSM8K 86.7%, MATH-500 83.7%, MMLU 92.6%, HumanEval 86.1%. Mean retention 87.3%.\n- Table 9 (Multi-seed): 29.1 ± 1.1pp across 3 seeds, 89-91% pruned-set overlap.\n- CKA reliability: split-half ρ=0.94, calibration 256/512/1024 yields 27.4/28.9/29.3%.\n\nMethod details:\n- Model: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, 32 layers × 28 heads = 896 total heads\n- CKA computed on 512 calibration samples, mean-pooled activations, linear CKA\n- Recovery: 3 epochs SFT on 7,473 GSM8K training examples (lr=2e-5), then 2,000 GRPO steps (G=8, lr=5e-7, max_completion_length=4096)\n- Evaluation: GSM8K 1,319 test samples, bootstrapped 95% CIs (10K iterations)\n- Baselines: Standard (Taylor importance), GRASPrune (ACL 2026, learned gates), Bonsai (ICML 2025, gradient-free), Random\n- α hyperparameter not explicitly tuned/discussed in paper\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full imagined experiment list includes 14 experiments (plan_001 through plan_014). The paper covers:\n- plan_001: Full GSM8K eval (covered)\n- plan_002: SFT warmup (covered)\n- plan_003: GRPO recovery (covered)\n- plan_004: Full eval on recovered models (covered)\n- plan_005: GRASPrune baseline (covered)\n- plan_006: Multi-seed robustness (covered)\n- plan_007: Scoring ablation (covered)\n- plan_008: Per-structure CKA-recoverability correlation (covered)\n- plan_009: SFT-only recovery comparison (covered)\n- plan_010: 25% sparsity (covered)\n- plan_011: 30% sparsity probe (covered)\n- plan_012: Extended evaluation (covered)\n- plan_013: Bonsai baseline (covered)\n- plan_014: CKA reliability analysis (covered)\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the RASP scoring formula well-motivated? Is CKA the right similarity metric? Is α discussed/tuned? Are there methodological gaps?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor**: Are experiments well-controlled? Are baselines fair? Are statistical tests appropriate? Are CIs meaningful? Any missing controls?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Are enough details provided? Hyperparameters? Code/data availability?\n\n4. **Weaknesses in Methods/Experiments**: What are the main methodological or experimental concerns? Be specific.\n\n5. **Strengths in Methods/Experiments**: What works well?\n\n6. **Questions for Authors**: List specific questions you'd want answered.\n\n7. **Score** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept) with justification.\n\nWrite your full review. Be rigorous but fair. Top ML venue standards (~25% acceptance rate)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3778,"cache_read_input_tokens":64066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3778},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b0567a5-9e40-4546-bac6-cc776d07ad03","uuid":"9046252a-c199-4646-a62d-030ce8d96b7e"}
